-- title:
--   Fricke-orthogonal diamond-fixed vectors are p-old modulo inertia coboundaries
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $M\ge 1$ and $p$ is a prime with $p\mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2\nmid M$ (`hp2`), and $M/p\neq 0$.
--
--   **Curves and Jacobians.** [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar M`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) is the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (in the sense of `laurentBaseChange`, the subfield of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image) of the function field of $X_1(M)$, and [`ModularCurve.JOne M`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) is the group of degree-zero divisor classes $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of that field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; similarly [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionFieldBar M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L123) and [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127) for a subgroup $H\le(\mathbb Z/M)^\times$. The subgroup $H$ is pinned by `hH`: a unit $u$ lies in $H$ if and only if its image under reduction $(\mathbb Z/M)^\times\to(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$ is trivial, so $H$ is exactly the kernel of that reduction; [`ModularCurve.infSubgroup p M H hpM`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L246) is the image of $H$ under the same reduction map.
--
--   **Standing inputs.** `hIn : ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll M` asserts that the Hecke input data `HeckeInputsOneAlong` holds at every prime for level $M$, and that for every $d$ coprime to $M$ there is an automorphism of the function field of $X_1(M)$ over $\mathbb Q$ satisfying `IsDiamondAut M d` together with an automorphism of `x1FunctionFieldBar M` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ which is a base-change automorphism of `diamondAut M d`. `hcomm : ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar M` asserts that the endomorphisms `heckeDiamondGenBar M` of `JOne M` — the Hecke operators `heckeOperatorOneBar M ℓ` at primes and the diamond endomorphisms `diamondOneBar M d` — commute pairwise. `hdeg : ModularCurve.JOne.DegeneracyPullbackInputs (M / p) M p` asserts the existence of a divisibility $(M/p)\cdot p\mid M$ together with `HeckeBetaOneDefined (M/p) p`, integrality of the level inclusion `x1LevelInclBar` and of the substitution map `x1LevelSubstBar` at $t=p$, the property `HasPrincipalDivisors` for `x1FunctionFieldBar M`, and the fundamental identity along each of those two algebra maps. Three further class hypotheses require [`AlgebraicCurve.HasPrincipalDivisors`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L217) over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ for `x1FunctionFieldBar M`, for `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` and for `xHFunctionFieldBar (M/p) (infSubgroup p M H hpM)`, i.e. that every nonzero function of each of these fields has a divisor, of degree zero.
--
--   **The place above $p$.** $P$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with `hP : P.LiesOverPrime p`, that is, $p$ is a non-unit of $P$; `P.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` denotes the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ of the inertia subgroup of the decomposition group of $P$.
--
--   **Roots of unity.** $\zeta:\mathbb N\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ satisfies `hζ`: $\zeta(n)$ is a primitive $p^n$-th root of unity for each $n$, and `hζp`: $\zeta(n+1)^p=\zeta(n)$, a compatible system.
--
--   **The pairing.** $e$ is a $\mathbb Z_p$-bilinear form on [`TateModule p (ModularCurve.JOne M)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15), the group of sequences $(x_n)$ in `JOne M` with $p^n x_n=0$ and $p\,x_{n+1}=x_n$. Hypothesis `he` reads $e$ as the divisorial Weil pairing through $\zeta$: for every $n$, every [`AlgebraicCurve.DivisorialWeilPairingData`](def/AlgebraicCurve_FunctionFieldWeilPairingDivisorial.html#L448) $W$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ for `x1FunctionFieldBar M` at level $p^n$, all $a,b$ in the Tate module and all $a',b'$ in the $p^n$-torsion [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.torsion`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L244) whose underlying classes are the $n$-th components of $a$ and of $b$ respectively, one has $W.\mathrm{pair}\,a'\,b'=\zeta(n)^{(e\,a\,b).\mathrm{appr}\,n}$, the exponent being the $n$-th natural-number approximant of the $p$-adic integer $e(a,b)$. Hypothesis `hskew` states $e(b,a)=-e(a,b)$, and `hnd` states that $e(a,b)=0$ for all $b$ forces $a=0$.
--
--   **The inclusion and the involutions.** $\iota$ is an algebra map `xHFunctionFieldBar M H →ₐ[AlgebraicClosure ℚ] x1FunctionFieldBar M`, pinned by `hι` to be the identity on the underlying Laurent series. Automorphisms $w_1$ of `x1FunctionFieldBar M` and $w_M$ of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ are given, subject to: `hw₁`, the endomorphism of [`TateModule p (JOne M)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) induced through [`TateModule.rep`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L174) by the semilinear automorphism `SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut w₁` is an involution; `hsq`, $\iota(w_M f)=w_1(\iota f)$ for all $f$; `hwM`, the action of `SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut wM` on `JH M H` is an involution; and `hwMI`, that action commutes with the action of every $\sigma\in$ `P.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` on `JH M H`. A further automorphism $w_{M,p}$ of `xHFunctionFieldBar (M/p) (infSubgroup p M H hpM)` is given with `hwMp`: its action on `JH (M/p) (infSubgroup p M H hpM)` is an involution. No property of $w_1$, $w_M$, $w_{M,p}$ beyond these is assumed.
--
--   **The exchange hypothesis `hexch`.** For every pair of algebra maps $\alpha_H,\beta_H$ from `xHFunctionFieldBar (M/p) (infSubgroup p M H hpM)` to `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` such that $\alpha_H$ is the identity on underlying Laurent series and $\beta_H$ acts on them by [`ModularCurve.qExpand (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) p`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25) (the substitution $q\mapsto q^p$), both integral as ring maps, both module-finite in the sense of [`AlgebraicCurve.FiniteAlong`](def/AlgebraicCurve_Correspondence.html#L37), and both satisfying the pushforward norm formula [`AlgebraicCurve.NormFormulaAlong`](def/AlgebraicCurve_Correspondence.html#L43), and for every $x$ in `JH M H`: the pushforward of $w_M\cdot x$ along $\beta_H$ equals $w_{M,p}$ applied to the pushforward of $x$ along $\alpha_H$, where pushforward means [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.pushforwardAlongHom`](def/Isogeny_ConditionalCurrency.html#L32) and the actions are those of the corresponding `SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut`.
--
--   **Conclusion.** With `JOne M` carrying the Hecke-algebra module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar M`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129) (which, since `hcomm` holds, is the one sending the Hecke generator at a prime $\ell$ to `heckeOperatorOneBar M ℓ` and the diamond generator at $d$ to `diamondOneBar M d`), the following holds for every $x$ in [`TateModule p (ModularCurve.JOne M)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15). Assume:
--
--   (i) *diamond-fixedness*: for every $n$ and every $d$ in [`ModularCurve.normFreeRepsAt M p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L21), the finset of $d<M$ coprime to $M$ with $d\equiv 1 \pmod{M/p}$, one has `diamondOneBar M d (xₙ) = xₙ`;
--
--   (ii) *orthogonality*: for every $s$ in the Tate module such that (a) for every $\tau\in$ `P.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` the Galois action of $\tau$ on $s$ is multiplication by the image in $\mathbb Z_p$ of the value $\chi(\tau)$ of the cyclotomic character `cyclotomicCharacter (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) p` at $\tau$, and (b) for every automorphism $\varphi$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\mathbb Q$ with `P.IsFrobeniusAt φ p` both $T(\varphi\cdot s)=\chi(\varphi)\cdot s$ and $\varphi\cdot(T s)=\chi(\varphi)\cdot s$, where $T$ is the operator [`ModularCurve.tateHeckeRepOne p (ModularCurve.JOne M) (ModularCurve.heckeGenOne ⟨p, _⟩)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L176) induced on the Tate module by the Hecke-algebra generator at $p$ — it follows that $e\bigl(x,\;w_1\cdot s\bigr)=0$, the second argument being the image of $s$ under the endomorphism induced by `SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut w₁`.
--
--   Then there exists $k\in\mathbb N$ such that $p^k\cdot x$ lies in the $\mathbb Z_p$-span of the union of the following two sets of elements $z$ of the Tate module:
--
--   — the *$p$-old* vectors: those $z$ for which there are $i\in\{0,1\}$ and $w$ in [`TateModule p (ModularCurve.JOne (M/p))`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) with $z_n=$ [`ModularCurve.JOne.degeneracyPullbackPair (M/p) M p i`](def/ModularCurve_X1DegeneracyPullback.html#L120) applied to $w_n$ for every $n$, the two maps in question being the pullbacks along the level inclusion and along the $q\mapsto q^p$ substitution provided by `hdeg`;
--
--   — the *inertia coboundaries of diamond-fixed vectors*: those $z$ for which there are $\tau\in$ `P.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` and $y$ in the Tate module with $y$ satisfying the same diamond-fixedness condition as in (i) and $z=\tau\cdot y-y$.
--
--   This is the lattice form of the orthogonality assertion about the Fricke-twisted $p$-adic Weil pairing on $T_p J_1(M)$ at a prime exactly dividing the level: a diamond-fixed vector annihilated by the pairing against every vector on which inertia acts by the cyclotomic character and the Hecke operator at $p$ interacts with Frobenius in the toric manner becomes, after multiplication by a power of $p$, a combination of $p$-old vectors and inertia coboundaries. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_bilinForm_tateModule_jOne_hecke_selfAdjoint_rep_diamond_cyclotomic_toricOrthogonal_mem_span_degeneracy_inertiaAugmentation_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_bilinForm_tateModule_jOne_hecke_selfAdjoint_rep_diamond_cyclotomic_toricOrthogonal_mem_span_degeneracy_inertiaAugmentation_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd), which packages the pairing and its properties into a single bilinear form, and thereby enters the level-lowering step at primes dividing the level once.
